module bindings.php.zip_member_write_without_containment;

// ZipArchive hands the extracting code whatever the archive declares:
// getNameIndex/locateName return member names, getFromName and getStream
// return member content, and a manifest stored inside the archive (a
// mapping.json, a package descriptor) can carry a destination name as
// content just as freely. PHP's concatenation joins such a value onto a
// destination directory with no semantics of its own -- `$base . $name`
// keeps every `..` component the name carries -- so a manual extraction
// loop that opens the joined path for writing lets the archive decide
// where the bytes land with the writing process's privileges (CWE-22).
// The convenience API is the other discipline: ZipArchive::extractTo
// strips absolute paths and `..` components itself, and is labelled
// code.ArchiveEntryWrite separately.
//
// The loop is recognised by its documented parts together: a member
// read -- getFromName, getStream, getNameIndex or locateName, the
// ZipArchive calls that hand out member names and content -- and the
// destination write pair, fopen plus fwrite or file_put_contents. A
// member read alone is a read-only walk (report imports, template
// parsers whose `getStream` is a token stream rather than an archive),
// and a write alone never touched the archive. The
// neutralising forms are the documented path reductions: basename()
// reduces a member-derived name to a single component, realpath()
// resolves the joined path against the filesystem. Either call in the
// function means the destination was reduced or checked before use,
// which is what the review flag asks about.
//
// Residual, stated rather than hidden: the containment test is by call
// name, so a project that hand-rolls a dot-segment rejection with
// str_contains() or a preg_ loop over segments is not recognised and
// the flag stands -- the reduction has to go through one of the
// documented path APIs to be visible from the function's context. And
// the member read is required in the same function as the write, so an
// extraction split across a reader method and a writer method reports
// nothing here (the taint route through a labelled member-read source
// would be the way to reach it; no source concept for archive member
// content exists yet). In the other direction, a function that writes
// files and then reads them back out of an archive it opened -- a
// fixture round-trip in a test -- matches all three clauses; measured
// across the corpus that is the one shape this fires on beside real
// extraction loops.
binding zipMemberWriteWithoutContainment {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "php" and containsAny(node.context.call, ["getFromName", "getStream", "getNameIndex", "locateName"]) and containsAny(node.context.call, ["fopen", "file_put_contents"]) and containsAny(node.context.call, ["fwrite", "file_put_contents"]) and not containsAny(node.context.call, ["basename", "realpath"])
  emit issue code.ManualZipEntryFileWrite at node
}
